<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · c. 1440–1690 (from Oba Ewuare to the civil war of the 1690s)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Benin</h1><p class="soc-sub">From Oba to Enslaved</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes.jpg" alt="Benin Bronzes"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Kingdom of Benin</a> ranked its people around a sacred king, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a>, who lived secluded in his palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a> and in principle owned the kingdom’s brass, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and coral. Birth decided the top: the royal line and the hereditary chiefs called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzama">Uzama</a>. Below them rank came mainly from office. Oba <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-the-great">Ewuare</a> (r. 1440–1473) is credited with creating two orders of chiefs, of the palace and of the town, whose titles the Oba conferred, and freeborn men rose towards them through the palace associations. Craft <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> worked for the court, most people farmed in villages run by their elders, and war captives and condemned criminals were enslaved. The evidence is the court’s oral tradition, written down above all by the Benin historian Jacob Egharevba from the 1930s, the reports of Portuguese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> visitors, including Olfert Dapper’s book of 1668, and the palace’s brass plaques.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> c. 1440–1690 (from Oba Ewuare to the civil war of the 1690s)</span><span><b>People</b> not known</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 7</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n">The Oba</span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:39%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-unk" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">The Uzama</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:54%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-unk" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Palace chiefs</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-unk" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Town chiefs</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:70%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-unk" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Royal craft guilds</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:85%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-most" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:100%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Free villagers</span><span class="soc-bar-s">Most people (the rest)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-enslaved soc-unk" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Enslaved people</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people; ranks no one has counted are dashed.</p></section>
<button type="button" class="kd-story soc-kd-open">Read the whole story</button>
<section class="soc-ranks" aria-label="The ranks, from the top"><article class="soc-rank soc-b-ruler" id="rank-1"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/ozolua" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ozolua.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Ruler</span> · <span>One ruler</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">The Oba <span class="soc-term">Oba, Omo n'Oba</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">The Oba was Benin’s king, and his people believed he was sacred.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> was a sacred king. The Edo held that the survival and prosperity of every family depended on him, and it was punishable by death to say that he ate, slept or washed like other men. He left the palace only for great ceremonies; in public, attendants supported his arms, and the palace plaques show him flanked by two of them. He was supreme commander in war and appointed the chiefs of the palace and the town. In principle he owned all brass, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and coral, and a chief wore coral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> only by his grant. Oba <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-the-great">Ewuare</a> (r. 1440–1473) rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a> with walls and broad streets, reorganised its government and, by tradition, fixed the succession on the Oba’s eldest son. Before he could reign, the heir had to bury his father with elaborate rites and set up an altar to him, with a commemorative brass head.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The throne passed within the royal line, in principle to the eldest son, but brothers fought for it: after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ozolua">Ozolua</a>’s death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esigie">Esigie</a> overthrew his brother Arhuaran. In the 17th century reigns grew short, the father-to-son line may have broken after Ohuan’s death in 1641, and the chiefs’ power grew.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> One man, with the royal family.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-the-great">Ewuare the Great</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ozolua">Ozolua</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esigie">Esigie</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ehengbuda">Ehengbuda</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-2"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">2</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">The Uzama <span class="soc-term">Uzama</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">The Uzama were chiefs who inherited their titles and crowned each new Oba.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzama">Uzama</a> were the senior hereditary chiefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, and their titles passed from father to son. Tradition links them to the council of elders that, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba dynasty</a>, invited a prince from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> to restore the kingship, and their leader was the Oliha. They kept the right to crown each new Oba. Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-the-great">Ewuare</a> they also chose him and could hold him in check. Ewuare broke their power to choose by fixing the succession on the eldest son, for whom he created the title of Edaiken, and by building up orders of chiefs whom he appointed himself. The change was not smooth: tradition says that his son Olua ruled amid much dissent, and that a council of chiefs governed for some three years before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ozolua">Ozolua</a> was made Oba in 1483. The Uzama kept their precedence and their part in the coronation, but their authority was much reduced.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Birth alone made an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzama">Uzama</a>: the titles were inherited. What the Uzama could lose was power, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-the-great">Ewuare</a>’s reforms showed.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count: a handful of hereditary title-holders and their households, in a kingdom whose population is not known.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzama">Uzama</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-3"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">3</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Palace chiefs <span class="soc-term">Eghaevbo n'Ogbe</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Palace chiefs looked after the Oba’s treasures and household, and the Oba chose them himself.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-the-great">Ewuare</a> is credited with creating the palace chiefs, the Eghaevbo n’Ogbe, as one of two orders of chiefs appointed by the Oba and answerable to him. They led the three palace associations, the Iwebo, the Iweguae and the Ibiwe, which between them kept the Oba’s regalia and wardrobe, served his person and ran the quarters of his wives and children; the craft <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> of the court were attached to these associations. Freeborn men of the capital served in these associations for small rewards, and in time the Oba might grant one of them a title, which did not pass to his sons. Like the town chiefs, the palace chiefs collected tribute, heard disputes and took part in the affairs of state. The palace they served was vast: Olfert Dapper’s book of 1668, compiled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> reports, compares it to the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haarlem">Haarlem</a>, with apartments for courtiers and galleries whose wooden pillars were covered with cast metal plaques.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Service in a palace association was the usual road to a palace title, which only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> could confer and which did not pass to the holder’s sons.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count: a small body of title-holders and their households.</p></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-4"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">4</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Town chiefs <span class="soc-term">Eghaevbo n'Ore</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Town chiefs collected tribute for the Oba, and their leader, the Iyase, led armies in war.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The town chiefs, the Eghaevbo n’Ore, formed the second order of appointed chiefs that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-the-great">Ewuare</a> is credited with creating. Their leader, the Iyase, is often described as the kingdom’s prime minister, and in war he and the Ezomo commanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a>’s armies under the king himself. Like the palace chiefs, the town chiefs were appointed by the Oba and answerable to him; they collected tribute, heard disputes and took part in the affairs of state, and their titles did not pass to their sons. In the towns he conquered, Ewuare replaced the old ruling families with chiefs of his own system. War chiefs who served well received leopard ornaments from the Oba, an emblem otherwise worn only by the king and his highest officials, and the palace plaques show chiefs and warriors ranked by their dress and emblems. Over the 17th century officials grew stronger as reigns grew shorter, and around 1689 the Iyase Ode led a revolt of officials that began years of civil war.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> conferred town titles, often on men who had proved themselves in war; a son did not inherit his father’s title.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count: a small body of title-holders and their households.</p></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-5"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">5</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Royal craft guilds <span class="soc-term">Igun Eronmwon (brass casters), Igun Ematon (blacksmiths), Igbesanmwan (ivory and wood carvers)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Benin’s brass casters made famous metal heads and plaques for the Oba’s palace.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-the-great">Ewuare</a> is credited with dividing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a> into separate quarters for different crafts, and its craftsmen were organised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> that worked for the court. The brass casters, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igun-eronmwon">Igun Eronmwon</a>, ranked highest among the guilds attached to the Iwebo palace association, followed by the blacksmiths and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and wood carvers. The casters lived in a quarter of their own under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a>’s direct control; their guild still works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igun-street">Igun Street</a>. By the lost-wax method they cast the commemorative heads for the royal altars and the plaques that covered the palace pillars, which record the court’s ranks in dress and emblems; much of their brass came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manilla">manillas</a>, the brass rings that Portuguese traders brought from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>. The blacksmiths forged swords and spearheads for the army. Since in general only the Oba could own works of brass and ivory, the guilds depended on him, and their work was finest when he was strong enough to employ many specialists. Membership usually passed from father to son.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Most members were born into their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>’s ward. A guild’s fortunes rose and fell with the power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> it served.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count: the guilds were a small part of the people of Benin City, whose own size is not known.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igun-eronmwon">Igun Eronmwon</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronze-plaques">Benin bronze plaques</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-6"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">6</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>Most people (the rest)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Free villagers</h3><p class="soc-kid">Most people in Benin were farmers who lived in villages led by their elders.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a>’s subjects were free farmers in the villages outside the capital, Edo-speaking in the heartland and of many other peoples in the conquered lands. They grew yams, whose first shoots were blessed each year at the royal festival of Ague, and other crops. A village was run by its elders, led by the oldest or most respected man, the odionwere, and sent tribute to the capital through the chiefs, who also settled its quarrels. When the Oba went to war, the village regiments, called up as needed, formed the bulk of his army, while the picked regiments of the capital stayed under arms. Tradition holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-the-great">Ewuare</a> introduced marks cut into the skin of the freeborn to set them apart from slaves. Debt could be met by pawning: a person, often a child, served a creditor until the loan was repaid, protected by kinship from being sold as a slave.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A freeborn man could move to the capital and enter a palace association, the first step towards a title, and war brought rewards. A crime could lead to execution or sale into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Everyone not counted in the other tiers: the great majority, most of them farmers in the villages. No rank of Benin was counted in this period, so this also takes in the chiefs’ households, the guilds and the enslaved, whose numbers are unknown.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-people">Edo people</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-enslaved" id="rank-7"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">7</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Enslaved</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Enslaved people</h3><p class="soc-kid">Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; many were captives from wars.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> was part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>’s order. Many of the enslaved were captives from the wars of expansion of the 15th and 16th centuries; others came as tribute from subject towns or as gifts to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a>, and criminals could be sold into slavery. They were owned by the Oba, by chiefs and by ordinary people, and many slaves marked a man’s standing. On the account of the historian Philip Igbafe, drawn mainly from the 19th century, they were the chiefs’ main labour force and also served as fighting men. Slaves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> were traded at the Oba Market in the capital, and captives were sold to Portuguese and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> traders, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, ivory and later cloth counted for more in Benin’s trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Tradition honours a slave named Edo, who saved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-the-great">Ewuare</a>’s life and gave the city its name. Condemned criminals and slaves could be put to death in royal rites; historians such as James Graham argue that European accounts exaggerated how often.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> People became slaves through capture in war, a criminal conviction or tribute from subject towns; the sources for this period say little about how any left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count: chiefs owned many slaves, but no source for this period counts them.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a></div></div></div></article></section>
<section class="soc-born" id="born" aria-labelledby="soc-born-t"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-born-t">Born into history</h2><p class="soc-born-lede">Where would you have stood? Chance places you on the ladder, as birth placed most people: weighted by how many each rank held.</p><button type="button" class="soc-born-btn" data-born="benin">Be born in the Kingdom of Benin</button><div class="soc-born-out" aria-live="polite"></div></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Moving up and down</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>’s ladder was steep at the top but open in the middle. The kingship and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzama">Uzama</a> titles passed by birth, while most chiefly titles were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a>’s to give, so a freeborn man could rise by long service in a palace association or by valour in war, which the Oba rewarded with leopard ornaments and other emblems of rank. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-the-great">Ewuare</a> used such appointments to build up chiefs loyal to himself, and in the towns he conquered he put his own men in place of the old rulers. Favour could raise a woman too: Idia, a wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ozolua">Oba Ozolua</a>, became the first Iyoba. Falls were as sharp. A prince who lost the struggle for the throne, as Arhuaran did against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esigie">Esigie</a>, lost everything, and criminals could be executed or sold. Over the 17th century the balance tilted towards the chiefs: after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ehengbuda">Ehengbuda</a>’s death in 1606 reigns grew shorter, officials increasingly controlled the army and trade, and around 1689 the Iyase Ode led the revolt that opened a civil war.</p></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Women on the ladder</h2><p>A woman’s rank followed her father’s and then her husband’s, and almost every title was held by men. The great exception was the Iyoba, the queen mother. Tradition says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esigie">Oba Esigie</a> created the title for his mother, Idia, who had helped him win the throne and the war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igala">Igala</a> in 1515–1516, and that he ended an older custom of putting the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a>’s mother to death. The Iyoba ranked with the senior chiefs, had her own palace at Uselu, whose town was attached to the title, and her own regiment, and she was the only woman who could take part in war; after her son’s installation she never saw him again, but advised him by messenger. The Oba’s many wives lived within the palace. Women also traded: tradition remembers Emotan, a market trader who helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-the-great">Ewuare</a> win back the throne. Priestesses served Olokun, the god of the sea.</p></section>
